c/ul\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003eAircraft on Site\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eEC121 Warning Star (“Willie Victor 2”): radar plane with spinning bottom dish and hand-turned top dish\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eOriginal equipment intact; crew bunks, galley, head, radio room, and radar operator stations visible inside\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003ePB4Y2: under active restoration; will move to the main museum side when complete\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eHistory: post-war fire bomber in Canada, ran off a runway into the ocean after fuel exhaustion, recovered and restored\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eEngines swapped back from turbo-supercharged (fire bomber spec) to original non-turbo units\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eRB57: British-designed reconnaissance variant with jet engines mounted inside the wings\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eRF84F: photo reconnaissance aircraft with camera windows in the nose, side air intakes\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eF100: undergoing restoration\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eF101 Voodoo: present in the hangar, slated for removal\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003eVisitor’s Family History Project\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eResearching great-grandfather, a Danish figure knighted twice (1932 and 1937)\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eVisited Copenhagen in March; delivered a letter to the king’s secretary, who replied\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eUsing AI tools to surface additional historical records\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eGreat-grandfather earned $220,000/year in the early 1930s; lost significant wealth in Cuba\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eLast wife was heir to the Montgomery Ward fortune\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eGoal: produce a documentary or film; connected to the museum through Julie, who shared photos\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003eNext Steps\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003e\n\u003cp data-action-item\u003e\u003cstrong\u003eReturn this weekend to remove wings from the plane going to Ypsilanti\u003c/strong\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eTeam coming back down specifically for the wing removal and related disassembly work.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"27:Tc51,"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"\u003ch3\u003eWillow Run Bomber Plant Tour\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eHistoric Ford-built B24 bomber plant, originally 1 mile long, running east-west\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eBuilding passed through three owners: US Government, Kaiser Fraser, General Motors\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eBuilt on a 1,500-acre Ford farm; Ford orchards extended to the west tree line\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eAll visible concrete is original; other areas heavily modified over the decades\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eWorkers paid in cash (many lacked bank accounts); vault door still on site in the GM annex\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003c/ul\u003e\n\u003ch3\u003eWartime Production Layout\u003c/h3\u003e\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eChain drag rails ran through the building, pulling planes through in sections\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eMultiple paint bays and metal prep bays in sequence, not one continuous conveyor\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eDistance from current balcony to the tree line equals the length of a B24\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eTurntables (four) positioned outside the building to shuffle finished planes sideways\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eNorden bomb sites stored in a concrete room under the balcony for protection\n\u003cul\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eNot every plane received one: some used radio triggers or missions didn’t require it\u003c/li\u003e\n\u003cli\u003eBurroughs factory (Hagerty and Plymouth Road) manufactured the Norden sites, continuing production into the Vietnam era\u003c/li\u003e\n\u00